<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- CBS copy of epsilon_crane_behavior_tree/behavior_trees/subtree_a2b_movement.xml,
     with every SwitchController node removed.

     On the new stack `trajectory_controller_a2b` has three would-be owners:
     `crane_bringup/sim.launch.py` spawns it ACTIVE, this subtree used to activate and
     deactivate it around every motion, and `crane_supervisor` treats itself as the only
     caller of /controller_manager/switch_controller: it lists the controller in its
     follow-mode set and polls list_controllers STRICT. A strict switch that finds the
     world already changed under it faults the supervisor, which in a planner-tuning
     harness reads as a planner failure. The controller is chained too: it commands
     crane_velocity_controller's reference interfaces, not hardware, so its activation
     state is not private between this tree and the machine.

     So the tree only follows the trajectory; the supervisor keeps sole ownership of
     activation. The epsilon original wrapped the sequence in a Fallback whose failure
     branch was a ForceFailure around one more deactivation, and with nothing left to
     deactivate that branch is empty, so the Fallback collapses and the approval
     decorator is the whole subtree.

     epsilon_crane_behavior_tree is a shared reference package and is not edited.

     ONE COMMANDER PER MOVE (issue 107). `trajectory_controller_a2b` takes a trajectory two
     ways: this action goal, and `~/joint_trajectory`, whose documented behaviour is "always
     replace old msg with new one". A live goal does not close the topic path, because JTC
     sets `subscriber_is_active_` in on_activate/on_deactivate only, so an MPC re-goaling by
     topic every 40 ms would run against this goal's `goal_time` and per-joint tolerances.
     Today the commander is this action, and `crane_mpc` is not wired to the controller:
     nothing remaps `~/joint_trajectory` onto /crane/mpc/horizon and the node's default
     `mode: shadow` publishes to `~/shadow_horizon`. Whoever lands that remap must take the
     goal out of this subtree in the same change, and bring a completion oracle with it: a
     topic driven trajectory carries no goal tolerance check at all, and that check was
     `crane_supervisor`'s job, which is archived. -->
<root BTCPP_format="4">
  <BehaviorTree ID="SubTreeCbsA2BMovement">
    <Decorator ID="GetUserApproval" topic="/gpio_controller/remote_ctrl_states"
      wait_rising_edge="False">
      <Action ID="FollowJointTrajectory"
        server_name="/trajectory_controller_a2b/follow_joint_trajectory"
        server_timeout="500"
        trajectory="{trajectory}"/>
    </Decorator>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
